These examples use the OpenBSD PF grammar documented in pf.conf(5).

pf.conf is an inactive, fully commented IPv4 gateway template. Adapt the
interface names, addresses and policy before installing any selected rules in
/etc/pf.conf. A block/pass policy in an example does not change the kernel's
unmatched default, and installing this package does not enable PF.
Run pfctl -nf /path/to/proposed.conf before loading a real configuration.

Translation uses the current pf.conf(5) syntax: match and pass rules with
nat-to, rdr-to, binat-to and af-to; there are no separate nat/rdr rules.
Bandwidth queueing (the "queue" keyword) is not supported on 5BSD yet and
rulesets that define queues are refused; use dummynet(4) pipes and queues
(dnpipe, dnqueue; see dnctl(8)) for traffic shaping.

The FreeBSD-era faq-example*, ackpri, queue* and spamd files used ALTQ and
pre-4.7 NAT/redirection syntax and have been removed.
